<article class="env-lemma" id="0GZF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GZF">Lemma <span data-tag="0GZF">24.34.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O}$ be as in Section <a href="/tag/0GZ8" data-tag="0GZ8">24.33</a>. Let $\varphi : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}$ be a homomorphism of differential graded $\mathcal{O}$-algebras which induces an isomorphism on cohomology sheaves, then the equivalence $D(\mathcal{A}, \text{d}) \to D(\mathcal{B}, \text{d})$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FTZ" data-tag="0FTZ">24.30.1</a> induces an equivalence $\mathit{QC}(\mathcal{A}, \text{d}) \to \mathit{QC}(\mathcal{B}, \text{d})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to show the following: given a morphism $U \to V$ of $\mathcal{C}$ and $M$ in $D(\mathcal{A}, \text{d})$ the following are equivalent 
    <p>
      <ol>
  <li><p>
$R\Gamma (V, M) \otimes _{\mathcal{A}(V)}^\mathbf {L} \mathcal{A}(U) \to \Gamma (U, M)$ is an isomorphism in $D(\mathcal{A}(U), \text{d})$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R\Gamma (V, M \otimes _\mathcal {A}^\mathbf {L} \mathcal{B}) \otimes _{\mathcal{B}(V)}^\mathbf {L} \mathcal{B}(U) \to \Gamma (U, M \otimes _\mathcal {A}^\mathbf {L} \mathcal{B})$ is an isomorphism in $D(\mathcal{B}(U), \text{d})$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
    <p>
       Since the topology on $\mathcal{C}$ is chaotic, this simply boils down to fact that $\mathcal{A}(U) \to \mathcal{B}(U)$ and $\mathcal{A}(V) \to \mathcal{B}(V)$ are quasi-isomorphisms. Details omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>